# Financial integrity

Treat every change in scope as critical risk.

- Provider events and commands must have stable idempotency keys and at-most-once database effects.
- Enforce idempotency with unique constraints and conditional state transitions, not pre-read checks alone.
- Concurrent delivery of the same webhook must produce the same final state and one ledger effect.
- One order has at most one active payment attempt unless an explicit expiry/retry policy allows another.
- Amount, currency, reference, provider, order ownership, and allowed prior state must be verified before settlement.
- Ledger entries are immutable and uniquely attributable to their source event, order, refund, or payout transition.
- Payout reserve, success, failure, and reversal transitions must conserve balance exactly once.
- Inventory reservation/release must be atomic, never negative, and safe under concurrent checkout/cancellation.
- Use decimal database values and documented boundary rounding; never use floating point as the money source of truth.
- Test success, rejection, duplicate sequential delivery, concurrent delivery, retry after partial failure, and reconciliation.
